WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.002
-Your life, your career
has obviously taken

00:00:03.002 --> 00:00:06.472
so many unexpected
but also well-deserved turns

00:00:06.472 --> 00:00:07.907
to the place where you are
obviously playing

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:10.209
a police officer on one of
my favorite shows,

00:00:10.209 --> 00:00:13.813
"Brooklyn Nine-Nine,"
a show with as diverse a cast

00:00:13.813 --> 00:00:16.082
on network television
that you can find.

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:18.284
And, yet, you guys are
playing police officers.

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:21.587
Do you expect that
the events of the day

00:00:21.587 --> 00:00:24.657
are going to affect
the next season?

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:28.060
-Definitely. You know,
we actually all got on

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.262
a Zoom call just the other day

00:00:30.262 --> 00:00:32.298
because of what's happening
in this country.

00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:39.138
And we were witnessing so many,
you know, just abuses of power.

00:00:39.138 --> 00:00:41.674
We had some somber talks

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:46.179
and some really,
really eye-opening conversation

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:49.348
about how to handle
this new season.

00:00:49.348 --> 00:00:53.553
-You tweeted something this week
that received some backlash.

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:56.122
Do you want to take a minute
to speak to it?

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:59.426
-Yeah. You know, one of
the big things that I tweeted

00:00:59.426 --> 00:01:02.361
was the fact that
I felt that, you know,

00:01:02.361 --> 00:01:07.867
defeating white supremacy
without the help of white people

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:10.769
could create a black supremacy.

00:01:10.769 --> 00:01:14.840
Now, the term "black supremacy"
was just destroyed.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.677
I mean, and what
I was trying to --

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:21.747
What I was trying to say is that
the fact that, you know,

00:01:21.747 --> 00:01:24.550
I, as a member of
the black community,

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:27.020
there have been
so-called "gatekeepers"

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.322
who decide who's black
and who's not.

00:01:30.322 --> 00:01:35.662
And in this effort to, you know,
to really push equality

00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:39.231
and to end white supremacy
and systematic racism,

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:42.435
there are certain black people
who have determined that

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:46.306
what I'm doing has no bearing.

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:50.710
I have been rendered moot
because I am successful.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:56.482
And my point is just the fact
that we need all of us.

00:01:56.482 --> 00:01:58.717
When you are talking about
women's rights,

00:01:58.717 --> 00:02:02.922
women's rights without men,
nothing changes.

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:08.127
If you don't -- If men don't
understand how to treat women,

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:09.295
we're going to have a problem.

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:11.897
And it's the same thing
with white people.

00:02:11.897 --> 00:02:14.233
If white people don't understand
how to treat us

00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:17.236
as a community,
we're going to have a problem.

00:02:17.236 --> 00:02:19.873
But also, in our own community,

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:22.040
we have to know
how to treat each other.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:25.578
And we have to allow ourselves
to agree, to disagree,

00:02:25.578 --> 00:02:29.748
to have different viewpoints,
because right now,

00:02:29.748 --> 00:02:31.283
in the words of Joe Biden,

00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:34.019
if you don't vote for me,
you ain't black.

00:02:34.019 --> 00:02:36.822
And these are terms that are
even passed around

00:02:36.822 --> 00:02:38.090
within our own community.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:41.127
And I'm going,
"Guys, it's bigger than that.

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:45.031
It's bigger than that.
We are bigger than that."

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:47.599
And that was the point I was
trying to make,

00:02:47.599 --> 00:02:52.171
but people held on to
the "black supremacy" concept.

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:55.507
And, again, it's Twitter,
and taken out of context,

00:02:55.507 --> 00:02:58.611
anybody can roll with anything.

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:00.913
So -- But I do want to
let everyone know

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:05.084
that that comment and that tweet
was said out of love,

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:08.388
in an effort for reconciliation.

00:03:08.388 --> 00:03:12.224
And I want to be the solution.
I do not want to be the problem.

00:03:12.224 --> 00:03:14.426
And I do understand
that we need all of us.

00:03:14.426 --> 00:03:18.297
We need every American
out there to contribute

00:03:18.297 --> 00:03:21.633
to this new watershed moment
that we have right now.

00:03:21.633 --> 00:03:23.269
I mean, this is an opportunity.

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:26.906
This is an opportunity,
and we can all get better.

00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:30.809
But I do not want to see us
get more and more extreme.

00:03:30.809 --> 00:03:32.945
That is the biggest, biggest --

00:03:32.945 --> 00:03:34.980
That's the biggest point
I'm trying to make.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:37.517
-I am often frustrated
by your many talents --

00:03:37.517 --> 00:03:40.720
a professional athlete, an
actor extraordinaire.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:41.821
And we've talked about
the fact that

00:03:41.821 --> 00:03:44.757
you are also a graphic artist.

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:46.225
You gave me a children's book

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:49.162
that you illustrated called
"Come Find Me,"

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:51.096
and it's one of
my kid's favorites.

00:03:51.096 --> 00:03:54.032
And you taught an online
Zoom art class

00:03:54.032 --> 00:03:55.267
during this lockdown.

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:57.136
-YouTube came to me
with this whole idea about

00:03:57.136 --> 00:03:58.771
"Celebrity Substitute."

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:01.941
And I got with a teacher
from Compton.

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:05.844
Her name was Rachel Wong,
and she teaches art in Compton.

00:04:05.844 --> 00:04:09.815
And I joined her class,
and it was the most exciting --

00:04:09.815 --> 00:04:12.218
To see the faces of
the students,

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:15.788
I mean, the bright smile and,
"Oh, my God, he's here!"

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:18.857
You know,
I just wanted to be a light.

00:04:18.857 --> 00:04:19.859
You know, I love art.

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:23.496
Art really saved my life
in so many ways,

00:04:23.496 --> 00:04:26.666
and I can see what that can do
for other people.

00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:28.801
And I can't wait to do it again.

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:30.369
I am actually, like,
trying to figure out

00:04:30.369 --> 00:04:33.172
a way to sneak in
some other classes.

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:34.607
-Well, there you go.

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:36.642
If you're an educator --
I know most school is over --

00:04:36.642 --> 00:04:39.244
but Terry Crews is available to
pop into your Zoom art class.

00:04:39.244 --> 00:04:42.247
-That sounds good.
-Hey, thanks for speaking to us.

00:04:42.247 --> 00:04:44.349
It's always such a pleasure
to see you, Terry.

00:04:44.349 --> 00:04:46.853
Stay healthy and well
and give our love to Rebecca.

00:04:46.853 --> 00:04:49.088
-Hey, love you, Seth.
Thank you so much, my man.

00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:50.089
-Alright. I'll see you soon.

